{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\i\fs40\cf6\cgrid0 Player's Option}{\fs40\cf6\cgrid0 \'99:
\par Combat & Tactics
\par 
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }{\fs36\cf6\cgrid0 Foreword
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Way back in issue #39 of }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Dragon\'ae}{\fs24\cgrid0  Magazine, I found a great article called }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Good Hits and Bad Misses}{\fs24\cgrid0 . My friends and I had been playing the AD&D\'ae
 game for a couple of years, and we took one look and adopted the article's critical hit and fumble system. We ignored every piece of advice about responsible use of the system and began using the critical tables in our next game.
\par \tab Our epic battles turned 
into bloodbaths. Our group of adventurers left a trail of dismemberment and sucking chest wounds in our wake. I distinctly recall one battle in which my character, a dwarven fighter named Hendel, had the unbelievable misfortune of losing one leg, an arm, 
and the other leg at the ankle. He still had 30 hit points left, so Hendel kept on battling, swinging his axe with great war-cries as he crawled along after his enemies. Talk about your suspended disbelief!
\par \tab Now it occurs to me that maybe, just maybe, even 
Conan (or Godzilla!) would have been incapacitated by these injuries. In fact, maybe Hendel, if role-played well, would have curled up into a ball and cried for his mother when he lost that first leg. I sure would have. But we had a great time with it, ev
en when fumbles led to friendly-fire decapitations and other such incidents.
\par \tab The point to all this is that any fantasy role-playing game has a pretty tough job in creating fast but semi-realistic combat rules, and the AD&D game, even with variant rules lik
e the critical hit system we ran amok with, is basically an abstract game. The }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Combat & Tactics}{\fs24\cgrid0  book is a compromise that adds some detail to combat\emdash not to make it more }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 realistic}{\fs24\cgrid0 
, but to make combat more }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 believable}{\fs24\cgrid0 . There are darn good reasons why people s
top fighting after they lose a couple of limbs, why 12th-level fighters don't turn their backs on guys with knives in their hands, and why people ought to be polite to angry folks pointing loaded crossbows at them.
\par \tab If you're one of those players who think
s, "Hey, it's only 1d4 points of damage, what do I care?" (and who hasn't, once in a while?) you'll find that this book's going to make you think twice. I can't think of a single example in all of fantasy literature where a character wasn't concerned abou
t someone trying to put a knife or arrow in him; why should your character be any different? }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Combat & Tactics}{\fs24\cgrid0 
 rewards common sense and quick thinking. Taking needless risks and making bad decisions can get a person killed in a fight. Don't you think your character would see things the same way?
\par 
\par 
\par \tab Rich Baker
\par \tab November, 1994
\par 
\par 
\par Before anyone ever thought about creating a role-playing game, there was a little set of rules called the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Chainmail\'99}{\fs24\cgrid0  game. With that slim booklet in hand, one could use miniature figure
s to conduct medieval battles, from sweeping conflicts in which huge armies of steel-clad men fought for honor and booty, to small bands of heroes storming formidable castles, to forces of elves taking up their bows against fearsome dragons.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Chainmail}{\fs24\cgrid0 
 rules were hardly the last word in historical accuracy, but they were easy to learn and easy to play. They also did a great job of conveying what it might have been like to see a medieval battle unfolding before you. The }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Chainmail}{
\fs24\cgrid0  game eventually gave rise to the first fantasy campaigns, but a set of miniatures rules is not a role-playing game, and it wasn't long before the D&D\'ae game, and later the AD&D game, came along to replace it. Nevertheless, the }{
\i\fs24\cgrid0 Chainmail}{\fs24\cgrid0  rules for tabletop combat remain at the root of the AD&D game.
\par \tab The AD&D game is about more than combat, but what fantasy adventure is complete without at least one pitched battle where the heroes prevail by the strength of their sword arms and the sharpness of their wits? The }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Combat & Tactics}{
\fs24\cgrid0  book 
is for anyone whose heart races (as mine does) at the thought of clashing arms; not just hack 'n slash, but heroic battles with swirling action, ringing steel, and eldritch flashes of magic. You won't find 20 pages of tables telling you exactly where a sw
o
rd blow lands in this book, but you will find plenty of ways to make combat more than a dice-rolling contest or an exercise in subtracting hit points from your character's total. If along the way you learn to stay away from fights unless you're sure your 
party can win them, that's even better.
\par 
\par 
\par Skip Williams
\par \tab November, 1994
