\par \tab Unless the DM wants to generate ability scores for every NPC in the game, it's safe to assume that any generic member of the race has the base movement rate listed. However, player chara
cters and exceptional NPCs may be able to move faster than normal.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Exceptional Abilities 
\par and Movement
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters with exceptional Strength or Dexterity scores can increase their base movement rate beyond the normal limits of their race. Similarly, characters with weaknesses in these areas are slower than others of their kind.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Strength}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Add the character's hit probability adjustment to her base movement rate. If you are also playing with the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Skills & Powers }{\fs24\cgrid0 
rulebook, add the hit probability adjustment based on the character's Strength/Stamina (not Strength/Muscle) instead.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Dexterity}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Add the character's reaction adjustment to his base movement rate. If you are also playing with the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Skills & Powers}{\fs24\cgrid0  rules, use the adjustment for Dexterity/Balance instead.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 For example, Loftos the Swift, a human fighter, has a Strength of 17 and a Dexterity of 16. His Strength gives him a hit probability bonus of +1, and his Dexterity gives him a reaction bonus of +1, too. His b
ase movement rate, therefore, is 12+1+1, or 14.
\par 
