\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Encumbrance}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Even the fastest sprinter won't move so quickly when he's carrying 140 pounds of armor, weapons, and adventuring gear. A character's encumbrance falls into five categories: }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 none, light, moderate, heavy, }{\fs24\cgrid0 and }{
\i\fs24\cgrid0 severe}{\fs24\cgrid0 . Encumbrance is described in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Handbook}{\fs24\cgrid0  in Chapter 6: Money and Equipment.
\par \tab To determine the character's encumbrance category, find his Strength and read across the table. The numbers on the 
table are the breakpoints for each category. A character with a Strength of 14 is not encumbered until he has 56 pounds of gear, lightly encumbered until he has 86 pounds of gear, moderately encumbered until he carries 116 pounds of gear, and heavily encu
mbered up to a load of 146 pounds.
\par 
\par \tab To calculate a monster's strength for this purpose, add 31/2 points per size category (rounded down) to the monster's base Hit Dice (ignoring plusses). Thus, an ogre has a generic Strength score of 18 (Large creature is 
size category 4, x 31/2 = 14, plus 4 Hit Dice = 18).
\par 
