\fs24\cgrid0 When surprised;
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 When faced by an obviously superior enemy force;
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 When an ally is slain by magic;
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 When 25% of their group has fallen;
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 When 50% of their group has fallen;
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 When their leader deserts or is killed;
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 When they are fighting an enemy that can't be hurt by their weapons;
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 When they are offered a chance to surrender and they've already met one other condition for a morale check.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Informal Morale Checks}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The DM can always just decide if a creature or NPC p
asses its morale check, without even rolling dice. The DM should be fair with this; it'll quickly annoy the players if their trusted henchmen run away through DM capriciousness at the first sign of trouble.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Formal Morale Checks}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par A formal morale check is a comparison of a 2d10 roll against the creature's base Morale rating, modified for the situation. An extensive list of modifiers appear on the DM Screen and under Morale in Chapter 9: Combat in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 
Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24\cgrid0 .
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Failing a Morale Check}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par When a charac
ter or group of monsters fails a morale check, their first consideration is to get away from the fight. If they fail by a small margin (say, 1 or 2 on the die roll), they try to withdraw in good order. If they fail by a large margin, they break off the fi
ght and flee for their lives. Intelligent creatures may try to surrender if there is no place for them to run, or if they think they won't be able to get away.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Status}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par This is a catch-all phrase for any condition or situation that may affect the actions nex
t round. Did a character get knocked down during the fight? He'll start the next round prone. Generally, magical effects and continuing damage (poison, burning, acid, etc.) actually inflict their damage in this part of the combat round.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12\cgrid0 Special Combat Conditions  }{\fs24\cgrid0 
There are a number of special situations that can arise in the course of a fight, through natural circumstances or military planning. Someone might fall down or be incapacitated by a spell. A battle fought in a thick, tangled forest may limit arch
ery to shots of 30 feet or less. Stinging snowstorms or sandstorms may limit visibility and carry away light missiles. The side that considers the characteristics of the battlefield in its tactics often gains an advantage over its enemies. Chapter Three d
escribes battlefields in great detail, but there are a few special rules that should be mentioned here.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Standard and Optional AD&D Rules}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par These rules can all be found in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Handbook}{\fs24\cgrid0  and/or the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24\cgrid0 . Even if they are listed as optional rules in the core rule books, they are a standard part of the }{
\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option}{\fs24\cgrid0  combat system. They are presented again here (with any necessary combat system alterations and additions) for ease of reference. Refer to Chapter 9: Combat in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 PHB }{\fs24\cgrid0 and }{
\i\fs24\cgrid0 DMG }{\fs24\cgrid0 for more details.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Movement and Footing}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Ground characteristics may drastically limit a character's ability to move in a fight. The movement reductions listed below apply to the character's base movement, so a character with a normal move of 12 spaces woul
d be reduced to a move of four spaces in heavy brush. If a character passes through multiple ground types in one round, he uses the worst of the modifiers.
\par 
