\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Cover and Concealment
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Concealment and cover work only against missile fire, and cover also provides a bonus to saving throw rolls where an effect causes physical damage, like a fireball spell.
\par \tab The amount of the target that is concealed or covered determines the penalty to any missile attacks made against the target.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keep\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1620\tx2880\tx3420\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b {\tab Target is:\tab Cover\tab Concealment
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1620\tx2880\tx3420\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\fs24\cgrid0 \tab 25% hidden\tab   \endash 2\tab \tab \endash 1
\par \tab 50% hidden\tab   \endash 4\tab \tab \endash 2
\par \tab 75% hidden\tab   \endash 7\tab \tab \endash 3
\par \tab 90% hidden\tab \endash 10\tab \tab \endash 4
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24\cgrid0 
\par A target is impossible to hit when it is completely blocked from the archer by cover.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Line of Fire. }{\fs24\cgrid0 
A figure's line of fire is considered to run from the center of its square to the center of the target's square. If the line of fire passes through any part of a square containing an obstacle, the target is considered to be covered or concealed to some ex
t
ent. The DM can decide whether the target is 25%, 50%, 75%, or 90% screened, or he can roll randomly if it is not clear. If the line of fire passes through a square containing a figure engaged in melee, the archer must use the rules for firing into a mele
e to determine where his shot goes.
\par \tab Note that intelligent creatures who are facing the archer will usually use the screening object for its maximum effect, while animals and monsters that aren't intelligent (Int 1 or less) will rarely try to screen themselves against an archer's fire.

\par \tab If there's any question about which squares the line of fire passes through, you can use a string, a ruler, or the edge of a card to check the missile's path.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Mounts}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Mounts provide their rider with a +1 bonus to attack rolls when fighting against unmounted opponents, while the opponents suffer a \endash 
1 penalty to attacks against the rider (there is no penalty to attack the mount itself). Most mounts are fairly large and can overrun smaller creatures. A normal human on horseback c
an use his mount's speed and size to trample his opponents underfoot (see Overrun). Many mounts are also capable of making attacks along with their rider.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Rear or Flank Attacks
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 If a creature is able to position itself for a rear or flank attack, it gains a significant advantage. Flank attacks get a +1 bonus to hit, and rear attacks a +2 bonus. A thief attacking from the rear can declare a }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 
backstab }{\fs24\cgrid0 and gain a +4 bonus to his attack roll. In addition to the attacker advantages, the defender's shield doesn't help against flank attacks on his unshielded side nor on }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 any }{\fs24\cgrid0 
rear attacks. Last but not least, the defender's Dexterity adjustment doesn't count against rear attacks, since the defender can't see the attack coming and attempt to dodge it.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Sitting, Kneeling, and Lying Prone
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters may sit, kneel, or fall prone as a no-move action. Getting up from sitting or kneeling is considered a half-move action, so a character can stand an
d still fire a missile or make an attack. Standing up from a prone position is treated as a full-move action, so the character can do nothing else in that round except rise.
\par \tab Sitting or kneeling characters are slightly harder to hit with missiles or thrown weapons; they gain an Armor Class bonus of \endash 
1 against any ranged attacks. However, they're at a disadvantage in melee. Anyone making a melee attack against a sitting or kneeling character gains a +2 bonus to hit them.
\par \tab Prone characters present very small missile targets and gain an AC bonus of \endash 2 versus ranged attacks. However, they are very vulnerable to anyone close enough to threaten them. Melee attacks against prone characters gain a +4 bonus to hit.

\par \tab Kneeling characters can use any weapon with no penalty. Sitting characters can only use crossbows without a penalty; with any other weapon, they suffer a \endash 
2 penalty to their attack rolls. (Note that characters on horseback are mounted, not sitting!) Prone characters can only use crossbows or size S weapons
 while they're on the ground. A prone character firing a crossbow attacks at one-half the normal rate of fire and makes any melee attacks with a \endash 4 penalty to hit.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Damage and Dying}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Creatures reduced to negative hit points are incapacitated and begin losing 1 hit point per round until they reach \endash 
10, at which point they die. A creature's loss of hit points can be halted by binding its wounds, using the healing proficiency, or casting some kind of curative magic on the victim.
\par \tab Characters who have been reduc
ed to 0 hit points or less are helpless; they can't tend their own wounds or take any actions. At exactly 0 hit points, the character is simply unconscious, and remains so until healed or until 2d6 full turns pass. If a character's death is prevented by b
inding his wounds or healing him, the character is completely helpless for at least 24 hours.
\par \tab It is dangerous to leave characters at a negative hit point total for long. Each day that a character begins with negative hit points, roll a d10 and compare it w
ith the absolute value of the character's hit points (in other words, take into account only the numerical value of the hit points, ignoring the negative sign). If the die roll is less than this numerical score, the character loses 1d4 additional hit poin
ts. This additional loss of hit points can be avoided if someone with the healing proficiency is there to tend to the patient, or if curative magic is used.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 \tab For example, Gorathan the Unlucky was badly mauled by a dire wolf and reduced to \endash 5 hit points. Hi
s companions bound his wounds, but no clerics or proficient healers were nearby, so Gorathan didn't recover any hit points. The next day, a d10 is rolled to see if he worsens or not. If the roll is a 4 or less (which is less than the "5" of Gorathan's 
\endash 5 hit points), Gorathan loses 1d4 additional hit points. If the roll is a 5 or better, Gorathan recovers hit points normally for a day of bed rest. If Gorathan has some bad rolls, he might not make it.
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Weapon Type vs. Armor Type}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Some weapons have an innate advantage against certain types of armor. In the Middle Ages, hundreds of weapons were designed for the purpose of penetrating heavy armor. Weapon types and armor types are explored in more detail in Chapter Seven.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Firing Into a Melee
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Throughout the ages, friendly fire has been a significant threat on the battlefield. In the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option }{\fs24\cgrid0 
combat system, the shorter combat round means that archers can't wait to pick and choose their shots. They have to fire or hold their fire in an instant.
\par \tab A melee is defined as any situation in which one creature threatens another. Firing or throwing missiles at either of the two engaged creatures requires the shooter to roll to see which figure he actually attacks. All creatures that are threatened o
r threatening one another in the same group are included, so the archer is guaranteed of at least firing at the right engagement.
\par \tab To determine the actual target, assign each Man-sized target 1 point on a die. Small-sized targets get 1/2 a point, Large targets 2 points, Huge targets 4 points, and Gargantuan targets 6 points.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Additional Rules}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par These rules are specific to the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option}{\fs24\cgrid0  combat system.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Higher Ground}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par If the attacker's waist is higher than his opponent's head, he gains a +1 bonus to his at
tack rolls. Stairways, tabletops, and steep slopes may create higher-ground opportunities for a character in combat. This doesn't apply to colossal creatures such as giants who are fighting on level ground against much shorter characters, nor does it appl
y to mounted characters; they already get a bonus.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Knockdowns
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Some creatures can smash their opponents to the ground with raw strength or heavy weaponry. Knockdowns are based on the size of the attacker's weapon compared to the size of the defender.
\par \tab Every weapon (including monster attacks) is assigned a }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 knockdown}{\fs24\cgrid0 
 die that is rolled when a hit is scored. Light weapons have a small die, while heavy weapons use a d10 or d12 for knockdowns. The size of the target determines what roll is required for a knockdown.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx720\tx1710\tx2160\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\fs24\cgrid0 \tab Target Size\tab Knockdown Roll
