\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\fs24\cgrid0 Duelists can also choose one of twelve types of attack:
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx630\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx630\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24\cgrid0 \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 1\tab Normal Attack: }{\fs24\cgrid0 
The duelist uses no unusual maneuvers, simply attempting to 
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 \tab \tab }{\fs24\cgrid0 land a normal blow with her weapon.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 2\tab Shield Punch: }{\fs24\cgrid0 The duelist allocates one of her attacks to performing a shield 
\par \tab \tab punch.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 3\tab Sap: }{\fs24\cgrid0 The duelist attempts to use her attack to knock her opponent unconscious.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 4\tab Pull/trip: }{\fs24\cgrid0 The duelist tries to use her weapon to hook or snare her opponent's legs 
\par \tab \tab and pull him off his feet. She must allocate an attack for this option.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 6\tab Offensive Disarm: }{\fs24\cgrid0 The character allocates an attack to the offensive disarm 
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 \tab \tab }{\fs24\cgrid0 option.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 7\tab Feint: }{\fs24\cgrid0 The character tries to draw out her opponent by faking an attack in one 
\par \tab \tab location and then attacking somewhere else. The feint is particularly effective 
\par \tab \tab against ripostes and disarms, and therefore provides a +1 bonus against those types 
\par \tab \tab of attacks.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 8\tab Outside Attack: }{\fs24\cgrid0 The character tries to attack around her opponent's guard, striking 
\par \tab \tab to the side of a shield or at a sliding opponent. This maneuver offers a +1 bonus 
\par \tab \tab against a shield block or a slide position. Flails are also very good for this and get a 
\par \tab \tab +1 attack bonus above and beyond any other bonuses for this maneuver.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 9\tab Riposte: }{\fs24\cgrid0 The character waits for her opponent to launch an attack and expose a 
\par \tab \tab weakness, and then she strikes in return. A character who ripostes must wait until 
\par \tab \tab after her opponent has attacked before she can make her own attack in that round. 
\par \tab \tab However, the riposte maneuver offers a +1 bonus to hit against smashes, wild 
\par \tab \tab swings, and outside attacks.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 10\tab Smash: }{\fs24\cgrid0 It's not elegant, but beating down a weaker character's defenses is a 
\par \tab \tab legitimate tactic in a fight. This type of maneuver offers a +1 bonus to hit versus 
\par \tab \tab blocks and parrys.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 11\tab Close Attack: }{\fs24\cgrid0 The duelist tries to get inside her opponent's guard and attack at 
\par \tab \tab close range. This maneuver provides a +1 bonus against opponents with larger 
\par \tab \tab weapons, but it cannot be used if the character's own weapon size is larger than her 
\par \tab \tab opponent's.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 12\tab Wild Swing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Foregoing all thought of defense, the character tries to make as many 
\par \tab \tab attacks as possible by raining a fusillade of blows on her opponent. Wild swings
\par \tab \tab gain a +1 bonus to attacks against normal defenses.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Ending a Duel}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Duels don't have to be fought to the death; in fact, there are lots of villains and monsters that would rather not get killed if it's possible to surrender and live. Some other common condi
tions for duels include fighting to first blood, fighting until someone is wounded (usually 25% or 50% loss of hit points), fighting until disarmed or knocked down, and any number of other alternatives. In social confrontations, the area's culture may hav
e dueling traditions that define the normal forms of combat and victory.
\par \tab For example, in Norse cultures, duels might be resolved in a }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 holmgang}{\fs24\cgrid0 , or island-going; the two parties involved meet on a small island, with no seconds or bystanders, and only one is
 permitted to return. Other duels may be considered over when the blood of one of the fighters stains a white sheet on which they fight, or when an impartial judge rules that one character or the other has been defeated.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12\cgrid0 Heroic Frays  }{\fs24\cgrid0 
The most heroic and inspirational stories of battle are about the stand of the few against the many. The legend of Roland holding the pass of Roncesvalles, or Davy Crockett at the Alamo, or Tolkien's tale of the Fellowship standing against the orcs of Mor
ia in Balin's tomb\emdash these stories live forever in the imaginations of people everywhere.
\par \tab In a heroic fray, the PCs are fighting against hordes of individually weak monsters such as goblins, kobolds, or giant rats. Warriors facing adversaries far less skillf
ul than themselves can double their normal rate of attack. This only applies to their primary weapon; if the character is fighting with a weapon in each hand, the secondary weapon still adds only one additional attack per round. Any attacks of opportunity
 the heroic warrior receives must be counted off against these additional attacks, however.
\par \tab In addition, warriors gain one extra attack per round which may be used to perform a shield-punch, shield-rush, unarmed punch or kick, or grab maneuver against any 
opponent that they threaten when they begin resolving their attacks for the round. The normal penalties for these attacks don't apply, so the shield maneuvers don't cost the warrior his AC bonus, and the unarmed maneuvers don't provoke attacks of opportun
ity.
\par \tab Characters can only engage in a heroic fray against creatures of 1\endash 
1 Hit Dice or less, or creatures whose Hit Dice or levels are 10 less than the hero's. A 12th-level fighter can declare a heroic fray against monsters of up to 2 HD, a 13th-level fighter can stand against 3-HD monsters, and so on.
\par \tab The other requirement of a heroic fray is numbers. The hero's side must be outnumbered by the enemy for the warrior to gain his extra attacks. If there are fewer monsters left than attacks available, the excess attacks are lost.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6\cgrid0 Chapter Three:
\par The Battlefield
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par For thousands of years, climate and terrain have played critical roles in the development of military tactics and the evolution of the battle. In flat, open lands, cavalry became the dominant force on
 the battlefield because the terrain favored mobility. But even the best horsemen lose most of their mobility in heavy forest, swamp, or rugged mountains. The commander who learns to use the terrain to his advantage becomes a formidable enemy.
\par \tab In many AD&
D games, the battlefield itself is often ignored or portrayed in a colorless, abstract manner. This chapter explores the various types of battlefields and their characteristics, with two goals in mind: creating more scenic and visual sites for your PCs' h
eroic encounters, and providing players and Dungeon Masters with another level of battlefield detail to reward quick thinking and sound tactics.
\par \tab The first section of this chapter, }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Battlefields}{\fs24\cgrid0 , discusses sites common to adventurers in role-playing situatio
ns and defines battlefield characteristics and terrain. Settings ranging from ice plains to tropical jungles are all included. The second part of this chapter, the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Battlefield Generation System}{\fs24\cgrid0 
, provides the DM with an easy way to quickly create a complete battlefield for any encounter. The last part of this chapter is }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Combat Under Unusual Conditions}{\fs24\cgrid0  and deals with a variety of strange situations.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12\cgrid0 Does This Belong in My Campaign?  }{\fs24\cgrid0 Even if you are not using the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option}{\fs24\cgrid0  combat system presented in Cha
pter One, you'll still find that most of the material in this chapter can be useful in any AD&D campaign. The information on movement, cover, and encounter ranges can go a long way toward making your battles more visual and exciting. Your players will als
o enjoy the new tactical opportunities (and problems) this chapter presents.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12\cgrid0 Battlefields  }{\fs24\cgrid0 Every fight the player characters participate in has to happen somewhere. Defining the setting for an encounter may provide players with ideas for ways to use the te
rrain against their enemies, or present the DM with an opportunity to make an encounter tougher by giving the monsters the same opportunities.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 The Four Basic Battlefields}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par In most fantasy role-playing games, battles tend to take place in one of four environments: a dungeon or cave, a town or building, a castle or fortification, or outside. There are exceptions, of course, but most battles occur in one of these environments.

\par \tab The DM can prepare four basic battle maps to represent each one of these areas, and then modify the map for the particular details of each new battle. For example, TSR's }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Dragon Strike}{\fs24\cgrid0 \'ae
 game includes maps of a town, a meadow, a dungeon, and a cave\emdash with a little work, the DM could use these generic maps to handle almost any fight.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Dungeons or Caves}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par There are three basic problems with fighting in a dungeon or cave: first, the fight usually takes place in a confined area; second, lighting may be a critical issue for characters who don't have infravision; and last but not least, the monsters 
usually know their own homes well. Unfortunately, most PCs end up going into dungeons or caves to confront monsters in their lairs.
\par \tab Overall, dungeons or caves may be the most dangerous places to have a fight. The lighting is poor, the footing is questiona
ble, and there are all kinds of possibilities for traps and ambushes. The cramped quarters eliminate all but the most rudimentary choices of tactics and strategy.
\par \tab When preparing a dungeon battle for the PCs, the DM should note the characteristics of the area in advance. Just about anything can live in a dungeon, and almost any kind of furniture or obstacles can be found there.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Town or Building
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 
Fighting inside buildings or in the streets of a town is almost as risky as fighting inside a dungeon. Ambush lurks around every corner, and at times it may be hard to distinguish between enemies and bystanders. Many town fights involve thieves or cutthro
ats who know their turf far better than the PCs, who are often visitors.
\par \tab The biggest consideration in a fight in a civilized area is the possibility of help or interference from the local watch or nosy bystanders. Unless the PCs are willing to accept a pri
ce on their heads, they'll want to cooperate with the local constabulary. The DM should make a point of preparing for the arrival of the watch, since it's likely that someone will call for help before too long.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Outside
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Battlefields in the great outdoors r
ange from featureless to impassable, depending on the terrain and ground cover. From the DM's point of view, an outside battle is a difficult one to run, since there aren't many maps of forests or hillsides around. A second consideration is the possibilit
y of long-range combat that becomes a melee, forcing a change of scale.
\par \tab The battlefield generation system in the next section of this chapter deals with battlefields in all kinds of terrain. Using this system, the DM can randomly create a detailed battlefield in no time at all.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Castles or Fortifications
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 It doesn't take a genius to realize that storming a castle or fort of some kind is dangerous and bloody work. The defenders have the advantage of full cover and deadly traps or devices to make short work of
 attackers. Even high-level characters should think twice before entering the killing zones of a well-designed gatehouse or keep.
\par \tab As with dungeons or buildings, the DM should prepare accurate and detailed maps, with notes about cover and trap locations, b
efore the gaming session. This will save a lot of time during the game and eliminate the half-hour pauses in the game while the DM sets up the fight.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Battlefield Characteristics
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 The characteristics of a battlefield can often change the nature of an encoun
ter by limiting movement, visibility, or missile fire. For example, charging is impossible in waist-deep water, and archery is ineffective in dense forest. Battlefields are described with six general characteristics: encounter range, lines of fire, cover,
 footing, obstacles, and materials or hazards that can be found there.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Encounter Range}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par This is the distance at which one group sights another group. The encounter range is governed by the ground cover and terrain; obviously, it's much harder to sneak up on
 someone on a flat plain than it is in heavy forest. Tactical encounter ranges are found in Chapter One under }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Opening the Battle}{\fs24\cgrid0 .
\par \tab These ranges may seem very short, but keep in mind the fact that these distances assume that both parties are trying to avoid
 being spotted. If one side or the other doesn't care about being seen, it's fair to double or triple the listed encounter ranges.
\par \tab Note that encounter range is always the shortest of two ranges: the visibility from the surrounding terrain, and the visibil
ity from the ambient light. A character may be able to see for miles on an open plain in daylight, but on a moonless night that open land is fairly well hidden. On a dark night in the forest, people can pass within five feet of a patient enemy without kno
wing that he's there.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Lines of Fire}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par This is related to encounter range, since terrain types that limit visibility also restrict missile fire. A battlefield's lines of fire are described as }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 clear, impaired}{\fs24\cgrid0 , or }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 severely impaired}{\fs24\cgrid0 
. Clear lines of fire are easy: the battlefield has no effects on missile fire.
\par \tab Impaired lines of fire have no effect on missile fire within the terrain's minimum encounter distance; for example, light forest has an encounter range of 4d6 squares, so the first 4 squares of any missile
 fire are unaffected. After this minimum distance, targets are treated as if they had one step of hard cover more than they actually do; a target in the open actually has 25% cover, 25% covered targets are bumped up to 50%, and so on. This is because low 
branches or trees are obscuring the line of fire.
\par \tab Severely impaired lines of fire have no effect on fire within the terrain's minimum encounter range, similar to impaired lines of fire, above. In severely impaired lines of fire, the maximum range of any mi
ssile fire is reduced to three times the minimum encounter range, and all targets in this area gain two levels of cover.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Cover and Concealment
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Most battlefields offe
r a fair amount of cover or concealment. This tends to be related to encounter range and lines of fire. Cover is simply described as available or unavailable; if there is cover present, its range from the character looking for cover is noted.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Footing}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The 
ground's movement characteristics can have a drastic effect on the course of a battle. This is referred to as footing. Footing is briefly described in Chapter One; the terrain descriptions that follow this section go into more detail on the footing for ea
ch type of battlefield.
\par \tab The principal effect of poor footing is to limit movement to a fraction of normal. A character with a normal movement rate of 9 who is in heavy brush is limited to a MV of 3. The character's full-move and half-move combat actions ar
e all affected by this limitation.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Obstacles
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Assuming that the basic battlefield is a flat, barren plain, obstacles are anything that interrupt a character's movement or provide cover against missile fire. They include streams, ponds, bogs, trees, thicket
s, dunes or drifts, slopes or bluffs, boulders, buildings, or walls.
\par \tab As noted above, obstacles have two basic effects: limiting movement and providing cover. For example, most characters have to go around a tree trunk and can stand behind it to use it for
 cover. If an obstacle just limits movement instead of blocking it completely, it can usually be crossed or climbed at the cost of a half-move for the character.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Unusual Materials or Hazards}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par This last category is a catch-all for items that just can't be d
escribed above. Some battlefields may have the makings for spears, lassoes, or slings just lying around. Other battlefields may have special hazards such as fires, quicksand, or water deep enough to drown in.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12\cgrid0 Terrain Types  }{\fs24\cgrid0 The following list of terrain t
ypes is not meant to be exhaustive; a book this size could be filled with descriptions of topography and ground cover for every conceivable battlefield. If you don't see something to match what you want, pick something close and approximate.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Badlands}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Badl
ands are rocky wastes or broken terrain with jagged escarpments, deep gulches, and little plant cover. The Black Hills of South Dakota and parts of the western United States fall into this category. Rugged, knife-edged hills tend to limit vision and confi
ne the fight to a small area.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 10d8 squares (50\endash 400 feet)
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Clear
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Random hard cover available within 1d6 squares of any character.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Rocky slopes reduce movement by 2/3; characters can move normally if they avoid climbing or descending hills.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Deep gulches and boulders.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Rocks for slings are handy everywhere. Falling down a rocky slope is dangerous\emdash if a character slips, is knocked down, or fails a Dexterity check while descending a slope, he rolls 10\endash 40 feet (1d4\'a5
10) and suffers 1d4 damage per 10 feet fallen.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Caves}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Adventurers seem to find fights in caves all the time. A typical cave is small, twisted, and dark, with uneven floors and hanging stalactites and sharp stalagmites. If the battle is taki
ng place in a colossal cavern, it's better to consider it an open field or hillside under total darkness.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 By range of visibility.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Impaired.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Hard cover is available within 1d3 spaces of any character.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Some cave floors may be muddy or slick, but generally footing is okay.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Rock formations and crevasses.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 If the lights go out, caves are pitch black. Fighting in total darkness gives the attacker a \endash 4 penalty on attack rolls and a random chance of attacking anyone engaged with him, including friends.
\par \tab Stalagmites can skewer an unfortunate character who falls on one; any character who is tripped or knocked down has a 1 in 10 chance of falling on a stalagmite. The stalagmite "attacks" with a 
THAC0 of 15 and inflicts 2d10 points of damage if it hits. A successful saving throw vs. paralyzation for half damage applies.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Desert
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 This is the classic sandy desert of motion picture fame. Most deserts are actually dry, rocky areas that are more like badlands or plains. The great dunes of a sandy desert tend to restrict vision and slow movement.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Longest missile range.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Clear.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Available by lying behind a dune crest, but none otherwise.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Climbing a dune face reduces a character to 1/3 movement.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Dunes block lines of sight and may shorten the encounter range. Dunes range from a couple of feet to several hundred feet in height and may stretch for miles.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Nothing of note.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Fields or Farmland}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Vast areas of civilized nations are farmland. Usually, cultivated fields indicate a settlement, homestead or village within a mile or two. I
n late summer and autumn, full-grown crops such as corn or wheat may be taller than a human and provide excellent concealment.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Winter/spring: long missile range. Summer or wild fields: 10d6 squares (50\endash 300 feet); autumn: 5d6 squares (25\endash 150 feet).
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Clear.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 None. Concealment is available in summer or fall by lying down.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Excellent, no penalties.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 None.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Nothing of note.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Forest, Heavy or Jungle}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par This is dark, dense forest with heavy undergrowth an
d closely spaced trees. The thick growth severely limits visibility and it is very easy to blunder into an enemy without ever knowing he was there. The Appalachian forests and the Pacific Northwest are good examples of heavy forest.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 2d6 squares (10\endash 60 feet)
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire:}{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0  }{\fs24\cgrid0 Severely impaired.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover:}{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0  }{\fs24\cgrid0 Hard cover within 1d3 squares of any character; concealment within 1d3\endash 1 squares of any character.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Heavy undergrowth and close-set trees reduce all movement by 1/3.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Trees and thickets.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 A ready supply of branches can provide clubs or sharpened stakes in no time at all. In some forests, heavy vines can be used for snares or lassoes.
\par \tab It's dangerous to ride at high speed in a forest; the rider of any mount moving at 
a run or gallop has a 1 in 6 chance per turn of being struck by a branch. The rider must roll a successful saving throw vs. paralyzation or be unhorsed, suffering 1d6 points of damage in the process.
\par \tab Dense forests severely restrict flying creatures of Size L or bigger. Such monsters must land in a clear area and approach their prey on the ground, since there is not enough space between the trees to unfurl their wings.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Forest, Light}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par \tab The principal difference between heavy forest and light forest is the amo
unt of underbrush. Light forests have much less brush to obstruct movement on the ground, and tend to be much airier and brighter. Small stands of trees, copses, or orchards can all be considered light forest.
\par \tab The ground level of a true rain forest can also be considered light forest, since there is little underbrush and the trees are spaced a fair distance apart. The canopy levels might be considered thickets or dense forest.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 4d6 squares (20\endash 120 feet)
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Impaired.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Hard cover available within 1d6 squares of any character; concealment available within 1d4 squares.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Normal.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Trees.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 See heavy forest.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Hills}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Most hills are considered to be part of another terrain. For example, gentle rolling hills are plains, forests, or farmlands\emdash 
their ground cover is their most important characteristic. This category actually refers to rugged foothills or highlands with light or no forestation. Large areas of the Appalachian mountains, the Scottish Highlands, and t
he foothills of the Alps fall into this category.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Determined by the level of forestation (heavy, light, or none.)
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Determined by the level of forestation.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Determined by the forestation, but in hills with no trees, hard cover is still available within 2d10 spaces of any character.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters moving uphill are slowed to 2/3 or 1/3 movement, depending on the severity of the grade. The forestation may limit movement even if the grade isn't too bad. Characters who s
lip or fall while moving downhill must roll a successful saving throw vs. death or roll 10\endash 40 feet (1d4x10), suffering 1d4 damage per 10 feet fallen.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Boulders, escarpments, and ravines or gulches.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters fighting with a height advantage (i.e., attacking from uphill) gain a +1 bonus to their attack rolls. Most hills are good places to find rocks for slingstones.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Marsh
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Marshes aren't swamps; ma
rshes are bogs or wetlands without trees. The ground is treacherous, and careless adventurers can easily become lost or mired in mud. Marshes may be flat, but tall stands of reeds can serve as excellent concealment, and characters who don't mind getting w
et can always go to ground to stay out of sight.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 4d12 squares (20\endash 240 feet)
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Clear.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 None. A character can find concealment within 2d6 squares behind reeds or in shallow ponds or creeks.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 In knee-deep water, characters move at 2/3 their normal rate. In waist-deep water, they move at 1/2 their normal rate. In shoulder-deep water, movement is reduced to 1/3 normal.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Ponds, creeks, and streams.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters grappling in water waist-deep or deeper can
 try to drown their opponent if they achieve a hold on him. The victim is considered to begin holding his breath on the round in which his attacker got his wrestling hold, and may begin to drown as described in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Handbook}{
\fs24\cgrid0 , Chapter 14: Time and Movement, under Holding Your Breath.
\par \tab Note that characters rendered unconscious by an attack may also be in danger of drowning if they fall into water of any depth.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Mountains
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Battles on actual mountainsides are rare. This type of terrain represents high pa
sses, canyon-walls, deep valleys, high snowfields, and windswept peaks. If the fight takes place below the treeline, consider the terrain to be hills, forest, or fields.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Long missile range.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Clear.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Hard cover is usually available within 3d10 squares of a character, but it may take mountaineering skills to get to it.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters moving uphill or downhill are reduced to 1/3 normal movement or possibly stopped altogether, depending on the grade. If no normal movement is possible, characters may decide to climb instead.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Boulders, ice or snow, cliffs, dangerous slopes.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters who slip or fall while climbing or moving downhill must roll a succcessful saving throw vs. death or fall 10\endash 
60 feet (1d6x10), suffering 1d6 points of damage per 10 feet fallen. Mountainsides usually have a plentiful supply of rocks of all sizes.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Plains}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Open plains are a tactician's greatest challenge. Without any clear advantage to be gained from contr
oling a terrain feature, battles become contests of maneuver and skill. Any commander can stand off a superior enemy force in rough terrain, but it takes a genius to defeat the same force on open ground. Plains include savannahs, dry steppes, and some gra
ssy deserts as well as American Midwest-style prairie.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Long missile range. A Man-sized creature can be spotted as far as 1,000 yards away on level plains.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Clear.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 None.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Normal.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 The random stream, gulch, or stand of trees.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Nothing of note.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Ships
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 In many AD&D campaigns, ships and boats are the fastest way to travel long distances. Many kingdom
s rely extensively on sea trade and are plagued by pirates or privateers. Naturally, adventurers tend to get involved in problems like this. Fighting on a ship first assumes that the attacker can catch and board his prey.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Belowdecks, encounter range is limited by the range of vision. Abovedecks, everything is in range for an encounter. Ships can spot other ships as far as 10\endash 
15 miles away, depending on their size and the prevailing visibility.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Impaired.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Masts, deckhouses, water-kegs, and gunwhales can all function as hard cover. Abovedecks, any character is within 1d4 squares of hard cover.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Normal. Climbing around in rigging is a different matter, though.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Masts, sails, deck cargo, and open hatches can obstruct movement.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 
The sails and masts of a ship are known as its rigging. Moving around in the rigging is treated like climbing, but there are always ropes or ladders handy. If a character in the rigging slips or is knocked down, he may fall to the deck or the sea.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Swamp
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Swamps are like marshes, but have trees and thickets. Most of a swamp's trees can be found on islands of solid ground, but many others (such as cypresses) can thrive in the water. The Louisiana bayou, the Great Dismal Swamp of 
Virginia, and portions of the Everglades are good examples of swamps.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 2d6 squares (10\endash 60 feet)
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Impaired.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Hard cover is available within 1d6 squares of any character. Concealment is available anywhere, by going to ground.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 In knee-deep water, characters move at 2/3 their normal rate. In waist-deep water, they move at 1/2 their normal rate. In shoulder-deep water, movement is reduced to 1/3 normal.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Trees, thickets, bogs, creeks, ponds, quicksand.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 See Marshes. In addition, swamps usually have wood or vines handy, which can be used to make improvised weapons.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Taverns
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 It seems to be the fate of adventurers everywhere to get into brawls and scrapes of the nastiest sort in alehouses and common room
s. The typical indoors fight is a dangerous and bloody affair, complicated by the presence of bystanders and the possible intervention of the town watch.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 By limit of visibility. Naturally, you can't fight with someone who isn't in the room.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Usually impaired.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Tables, benches, and furniture can all be used for hard cover.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Normal.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Furniture.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Chairs, mugs, bottles, and other tavern accessories can all be used as clubs or other weapons.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Town Streets
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 If the PCs aren't getting into barroom brawls, they're probably fighting in the alleys or streets right outside. Most medieval towns had very close-spaced buildings with dark, winding streets.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Encounter Range: }{\fs24\cgrid0 2d6 squares (10\endash 60 feet)
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Lines of Fire: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Usually clear.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Cover: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Hard cover is available within 1d6 squares of any character.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Footing: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Normal.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Obstacles: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Carts, wagons, vendor's stalls, passers-by, buildings.
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Unusual: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Walls and gatehouses often limited movement between sections of a town. Th
ese areas could be used as strongpoints by a defending force. Almost any kind of weapon materials may be close at hand in a typical town street.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12\cgrid0 Generating a Battlefield  }{\fs24\cgrid0 Since the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option}{\fs24\cgrid0  combat system relies on a figure's positioning and facing,
 any battlefield the DM creates should show obstacles and terrain features in the exact squares they occupy. The following section describes a quick battlefield generation system that the DM can use when he doesn't have the time or forewarning to prepare 
a detailed battlefield before the player characters get into a fight.
\par \tab This system should not replace old-fashioned preparation for an adventure. If you know that the players are likely to encounter a troll ambush in a mountain pass, make some notes about t
he setting of the battle before the adventure begins. Any staged encounters in buildings should be described, too. If you take the time to draw a good dungeon map, the map can be copied to a larger size for use with the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option}{
\fs24\cgrid0  combat system without too much trouble.
\par \tab The only times you should have to create a battlefield from scratch are when you don't expect a fight to occur, such as when the PCs run into a random encounter while traveling cross-country or pick a fight with someone they were just 
supposed to talk to. Save yourself the trouble of creating a battlefield if it is fairly obvious that the encounter won't lead to a fight or if it seems likely that the actual fight will be very short.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Step One: Scale}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Here's a tip for saving time: don't m
ake a battlefield bigger than it has to be. There's no point in mapping every street of a town if you're confident that you can contain the battle in one dark alleyway. Refer to the encounter ranges listed for the various terrain types in the previous sec
t
ion; you probably don't need to make the battlefield bigger than about 125% to 150% of the maximum encounter range. For example, in light forest, the encounter range is 4d6 squares. The maximum encounter range is 24 squares, so you'll want a battlefield a
bout 30 to 35 squares across. If the encounter is taking place indoors, it's not necessary to map out more than the room the PCs are fighting in.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Step Two: Topography
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Take your battlefield and divide it into nine sections, like a tic-tac-toe board. The lay
 of the land may place some parts of the battlefield higher than the other parts. Each of these nine sections is assigned a height relative to the others; from these different heights, you'll derive the slopes of your battlefield. (If this is an indoors f
ight, ignore this step.)
\par \tab First, determine the base topography for your battlefield: flat, hilly, or broken. Marshes, swamps, plains, and some fields and forests are flat; hills, deserts, and most other fields and forests are hilly; and badlands, caves, som
e deserts, and mountains are broken.
\par 
