\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Flat
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 No slopes or elevations worth noting. You're done with this step.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Hilly}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Roll a d6 for eac
h section of the battlefield; on a 1, that section is elevated 20 feet above the rest of the battlefield, and all adjacent sections are elevated 10 feet above the base height. On a roll of 2, that section is elevated 10 feet above the base height.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Broken
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Roll a d6 for each section of the battlefield; on a 1, that section is elevated 20 feet, but adjacent areas aren't elevated as they are in hilly terrain. On a 2 or 3, that section is elevated 10 feet.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Slopes and Escarpments}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Hills are surrounded by slopes. Each 10 feet of elevation requires a slope of two to four squares to surround it. Draw a hilltop of three to ten squares at the midpoint of each elevated area, and then circle the hilltop with a ring of sloping squares.

\par \tab Broken areas may be surrounded by slopes or escarpments. Any side of an elevated area has a 50% chance of being either a slope or an escarpment. If it is a slope, draw it as described above. If it is an escarpment, consider it to be one square wide.

\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Step Three: Ground Cover and Water
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Now, take each section of the map and determine how dense the ground cover is in that area. The categories are clear (C), thickets or brambles (T), light woods (L), and heavy woods (H). Roll a d6 for each area and consult the chart below:

\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx1530\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24\cgrid0 \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 1\tab 2\tab 3\tab 4\tab 5\tab 6
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Light Woods\tab C\tab C\tab T\tab L\tab L\tab H
\par Heavy Woods\tab C\tab T\tab L\tab H\tab H\tab H
\par Swamp\tab C\tab C\tab T\tab T\tab L\tab L
\par Hills\tab C\tab C\tab C\tab C\tab T\tab L
\par }\pard\plain \s1\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx1530\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel0\adjustright {Plains\tab C\tab C\tab C\tab C\tab C\tab T
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Clear
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 No ground cover in that section of the battlefield.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Thickets
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Brambles or thickets cover that section of the battlefield. Thickets tend to be in clumps; take about two-thirds of the squares in that area and mark a couple of bramble patches.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Light Woods
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Light forest is prevalent in that area of the battlefield. Trees are about three squares away from each other; mark trees in about one-quarter of the squares in that section.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Heavy Woods
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Heavy woods are like light woods, but the trees are closer together. Mark trees in about one-third to one-half of the squares in that area, about one to two squares away from each other.
\par 
\par \tab Standing water or bogs can be handled in much the same way as ground cover. Each section of a marsh, swamp, field, or plain may be clear (C), or it may have a pond (P), bog (B), or stream (S) in it. Roll 1d6 for each ninth of the map:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\fs24\cgrid0 \tab 1\tab 2\tab 3\tab 4\tab 5\tab 6
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Marsh\tab C\tab S\tab B\tab B\tab P\tab P
\par Swamp\tab C\tab S\tab B\tab B\tab B\tab P
\par Field\tab C\tab C\tab C\tab C\tab S\tab P
\par Plain\tab C\tab C\tab C\tab C\tab C\tab S
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Clear}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par No water in that region.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Bog
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 
About one-half of the area in question is a soft, muddy bog. Bogs are usually knee-deep to shoulder-deep water, with lots of vegetation, mud, and debris. Bogs tend to be one large bog, and not many smaller bogs, so mark only one or two distinct bogs per a
rea of the map. If two adjacent areas have a bog, they might be one larger bog. Consider connecting them together.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Stream
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Most streams are only one or two squares wide, and range from knee-deep to shoulder-deep. Streams don't usually appear and disappear w
ithout a body of water to flow into or out of, so make sure that the stream squares form a continuous line and go somewhere if at all possible.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Pond}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par About three-quarters to all of the area is under water. Ponds are usually knee-deep or waist-deep near the edges, but can be 10 or 15 feet deep in the middle.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Step Four: Obstacles}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Battlefields are often littered with obstacles and obstructions. They provide cover, limit movement, and occasionally endanger the people fighting around them. The DM should arbitra
rily scatter a handful of obstacles of his choice over the map. Obstacles include:
\par 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 Gulches, ravines, or ditches (any terrain except marsh or swamp)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 Boulders (hills, mountains, badlands)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 Rockfalls (hills, mountains, badlands)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 Deadfalls (forests, swamp)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnindent360 {\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls1\adjustright {
\fs24\cgrid0 Fences, earthworks, or buildings (any civilized area)
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Step Five: Putting It All Together
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Your battlefield should now be complete. Chances are, you have some defensible terrain and some open areas that invite attack. Now, the DM should decide w
here each force starts. If one side surprises the other, the surprised fellows have to set up first, and the surprising force gets to set up around them. If one side was stationary or defending, they set up along one randomly-determined map edge, and the 
attackers set up opposite them.
\par \tab If both forces were moving, roll randomly to see which edge of the mapboard each force was coming from. They can enter the encounter area from adjacent sides of the battle map, but not from the same side. If the players have
 a set marching order, the DM can insist that they set up their figures accordingly. Or, if the circumstances dictate, he can allow them to place their figures wherever they see fit.
\par \tab Please note that the simple creation of a battlefield does not guarantee a more exciting and interesting encounter\emdash 
the DM has to reward characters who use their heads by making use of the terrain, and present the party with enemies who do the same. Even a grizzled party of adventurers should think twice before going up a rocky
 hillside against a band of orcs who can see them coming.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12\cgrid0 Fighting in Unusual Conditions  }{\fs24\cgrid0 Since adventures are notoriously unpredictable, a surprising number of battles take place in the most inconvenient places. Player characters tend to get into fights 
while hanging on the side of a cliff, swimming in subterranean lakes, or while venturing into fantastic planes of existence. Last but not least, many magical spells can create strange effects. This next section addresses the most common of these unusual c
onditions.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Limited Visibility}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Fighting at night or in dark settings is difficult\emdash especially if your foe can see better than you can. As noted in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 DMG}{\fs24\cgrid0 , c
haracters fighting in darkness or heavy fog suffer penalties to their attack rolls, damage bonuses, and Armor Classes.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Moonlight or Moderate Fog or Rain
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters suffer a \endash 1 penalty to attack rolls and saving throws. Since nothing can be seen more than 100 yards (60 squares) away, spell and missile ranges are limited; no missile scale combat can take place in an encounter.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Starlight or Dense Fog or Heavy Rain}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Characters suffer a \endash 3 to attack rolls and saving throws, a \endash 2 penalty to AC, and only get 1/2 
their normal damage bonus for exceptional Strength. Nothing can be seen more than 50 yards away (10 yards for dense fog or cloudy moonless nights), so spell and missile ranges are very limited.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Total Darkness
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters suffer a \endash 4 penalty to attacks, saves, and AC and receive no damage bonus for exceptional strength. No special attacks such as disarms, backstabs, or traps are allowed\emdash 
the character can't see his own weapon, let alone his target.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Water}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Underwater combat is described in some detail in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 DMG}{\fs24\cgrid0 . Here's a quick reminder of some of the difficulties of underwater combat.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Weapon Restrictions}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Only type P weapons can be used effectively underwater; the water offers too much resistance to use any other kind of weapon except nets.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Vision
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Under the b
est of circumstances, characters can see no more than 100 feet underwater (50 feet in fresh water.) For each 10 feet they descend, this range of vision is reduced by 10 feet; if a character is 30 feet underneath the surface of a lake, he can only see 20 f
eet. If it is dark outside or the water is muddy, this could be reduced even more.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Movement
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters moving about underwater have to swim. See Swimming under Chapter 14: Time and Movement in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Handbook}{\fs24\cgrid0 .
\par \tab Characters who are only wading are much
 less affected. Wading in knee-deep water reduces movement by 1/3. Wading in waist-deep water reduces movement by 1/2 and adds a one-phase initiative penalty to any action the character takes. Wading in shoulder-deep water reduces movement by 2/3, adds a 
two-phase penalty, and causes the character to lose any Dexterity adjustment to his Armor Class (although it does provide cover against someone firing missiles at the character).
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Fighting Underwater Monsters From the Surface}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par It's always great fun when a w
ading character gets attacked by some aquatic horror. When a character fights against something that uses water for cover, slashing and bludgeoning weapons are useless. Only type P weapons can be used to attack underwater creatures, and there is a 
\endash 2 penalty to hit. The wading character also loses any Dexterity adjustment to AC when attacked from beneath the surface.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Climbing}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Cliffs and mountaintops are another awkward place for a fight. Adventurers seem to be drawn to the worst locations imaginable for their heroic battles and often find themselves under attack while they're clinging to a vertical wall of rock.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Movement
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Climbing characters normally move 1 foot per round for each point of normal movement. A dwarf with an adjusted movement rate of 5 can climb 5 feet per round in good conditions. Refer to Climbing under Chapter 14: Time and Movement in the }{
\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Handbook}{\fs24\cgrid0 .
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Fighting}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Climbing characters lose all AC bonuses for Dexterity and shield, and may have rear attack modifiers applied against them. They have a \endash 
2 penalty to attack, damage, and saving throw rolls. If a character is above his enemy, he gains a +2 bonus on his attack rolls; if he is beneath his enemy, he suffers an additional \endash 2 penalty.
\par \tab Any time a character is struck while climbing, she must make a climbing check, detailed in Chapter 14: Time and Movement in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 PHB}{\fs24\cgrid0 . Note: you can use a default check of 30% in good conditions.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Fighting Flying Creatures
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Encountering angry giant eagles while clinging to a cliff is usually no fun at all. Unless they can hover, flying monsters can only make one pass every other round to attack climbing characters. If the PCs are facing
 the cliff (most climbers have to), the flying creatures get the benefit of rear attacks. Intelligent flyers may try to use their talons to drag a climber off the cliff; treat this as a grab attack. This forces a climbing check, and even if the character 
succeeds, he still must win the Strength contest to stay on the cliff side.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Aerial Combat
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Aerial combat is described at length in Chapter 9: Combat under the optional rule Aerial Combat in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24\cgrid0 . Here's the gist of how these rules work in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option}{
\fs24\cgrid0  combat system.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Initiative
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 The more maneuverable flyer gains a \endash 1 bonus to its initiative roll for each level of difference in Maneuverability Class between itself and its opponent. Creatures at home in the air have an easier time fighting while airborne.

\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Threatening}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par No aerial combatant threatens any adjacent creatures. Combat in the air just doesn't work that way; battles tend to take the form of joust-like passes at each other.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Movement
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 All fliers with a MC of B or worse must decla
re a half-move action every round just to stay in the air. Class B fliers can hover in position, but fliers with a MC of C or worse must move forward in this half-move to stay aloft. This requirement to keep moving means that a class C flier can make an a
ttack against a stationary target once per two rounds, a class D flier once per three rounds, and a class E flier once per six rounds.
\par \tab Gaining a point of altitude costs an additional movement point; a flier with a move of 18 could move 9 squares forward an
d 9 squares up with a full-move action. Diving allows the flier to add one point of free movement for each point of altitude it drops, so the flier with the 18 movement could move 36 squares in a full-move dive. Of course, it also has to drop 18 points of
 altitude (90 feet) to do this.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Attacks from Below}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Creatures attacking from beneath an opponent cannot charge, but they may be in a blind spot. The defender can reply with an attack only if it has natural weapons or its rider is equipped with a size L weapon, such as a lance.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Attacks from Above}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Creatures 
attacking from above may declare a charge by diving at their victims. Lances, spears, and a flier's natural talons or claws inflict double damage in a dive attack. In addition, the defender may not be able to reply to the attack if its only natural weapon
s are talons or hooves.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Unseating a Rider}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Characters riding a flying mount can be "unhorsed," just like characters on the ground. If the character is strapped into his saddle (a wise precaution), he is simply knocked off-balance in his seat and suffers the
 same penalties as if he had actually been knocked down on the ground\emdash he is easier to hit and has to spend an action getting back in his seat.
\par \tab Naturally, if a character is not strapped in, he risks the damage of a fall if he is unseated by any means.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Combat on Other Planes}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Almost any conditions imaginable may be encountered in the various planes of existence. The Planescape\'99 campaign setting describes the general conditions of the various Outer and Inner Planes, but fights are also likely to occur in two 
other settings: the Astral and the Ethereal Planes.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Astral Combat}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par On the Astral Plane, a creature's tactical movement is based on its Intelligence score. Its move becomes 30 feet (6 squares) per point of Intelligence. In addition, Intelligence replaces St
ength for purposes of determining attack and damage adjustments, and Wisdom replaces Dexterity for AC adjustments and missile fire. All missile ranges are doubled, but non-native characters suffer a \endash 2 penalty to hit with missile attacks.
\par \tab There are very f
ew obstructions in the Astral Plane, and the encounter range is normally twice the longest missile range. Since this is most likely a great distance, astral combat should be run in missile scale. Fighting in the Astral is like aerial combat; creatures do 
not threaten each other, and can move in three dimensions.
\par \tab If a character's astral self is killed, she immediately returns to her physical body with only 1 hit point. If the character is killed while physically on the plane, she is dead.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Ethereal Combat}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Ethereal creatures move at their normal unencumbered rate, regardless of their equipment and armor. Unlike the Astral Plane, the Ethereal is a swirling mass of misty proto-matter that obscures vision; the Encounter Range is 10\endash 40 squares (50
\endash 200 feet). Missiles tend to be slowed by the misty matter, and shots of medium range or longer just stop and hang in the air.
\par \tab If a character is in the Border Ethereal, she can still perceive the shadowy outlines of the plane she just left. She can move through any objec
ts or creatures without impediment. Of course, she is unable to affect things on the plane she is viewing, but she can only be affected by the magical gazes of a very limited number of monsters.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12\cgrid0 The Effects of Magic on the Battlefield  }{\fs24\cgrid0 Needless to say, th
e one thing that breaks all the rules on a battlefield is magic. Spells and magical items can create almost any condition imaginable, ranging from the merely annoying to the truly catastrophic. We could spend most of this book describing the exact effect 
of each and every spell and magical item in the game system, but this wouldn't be very practical. Instead, we'll discuss the most common effects that may give DMs trouble.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Wizard Spells}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The most powerful and effective spells in the AD&D game are found in t
he hands of wizards. In fantasy role-playing, wizards often dominate battlefields like heavy artillery or airstrikes in modern warfare. Entire battles often hinge on one character's choice of spells and the enemy's attempts to down the wizard before he wr
eaks havoc on them.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Blink}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The spell }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 blink }{\fs24\cgrid0 works slightly differently in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option}{\fs24\cgrid0  combat system. Roll 1d6; on a 1
, the wizard blinks in the very fast phase, on a 2, fast, on a 3 or 4, average, on a 5, slow, and on a 6, very slow. The wizard's blink always occurs first in the phase, before any other actions are resolved.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Darkness, 15' Radius}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Creatures outside the area of darkness cannot trace a line of fire through the darkness to a target on the other side. Creatures moving in total darkness are reduced to 1/3 their normal move unless they wish to risk stumbling
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Enlarge}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par A character who grows to eight feet in height 
becomes size Large; a character who grows to 12 feet in height becomes size Huge. This affects reach, threatening, and moving through smaller figures; see Chapter One. Very large monsters can be made smaller by applying the reverse of this spell.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Fog Cloud
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 As with }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 darkness}{\fs24\cgrid0 
, magical fog blocks any lines of fire. Creatures blundering through the fog can easily be ambushed by enemies waiting for them to come into the open; the DM can insist on surprise checks if he deems it appropriate.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Hallucinatory Terrain}{\i\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 
This spell is very dangerous when used to conceal hazardous areas. If the victims do not perceive the illusion, they fall prey to whatever hazards the terrain holds. For example, creatures who run down a hillside believing it to be level ground receive no
 Dexterity check or saving throw to keep their balance; they automatically fall and roll or slide to the bottom of the hill.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Haste}{\i\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Creatures under the influence of a }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 haste}{\fs24\cgrid0  spell increase their base initiative and weapon speeds by two phases and always win
 initiative. Their movement rates are doubled, and they gain a second set of attacks in the very slow phase.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Invisibility
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 The best thing to do with an invisble character or monster in a fight is to secretly note its location each round and physically remo
ve the figure from the board. Do yourself a favor and don't run combats with invisble figures on both sides.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Jump}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The creature empowered by this spell can actually leap over Man-sized targets without provoking an attack of opportunity. In addition, if the
 jumper wins initiative, he can make a half-move action to leap away after he attacks an adjacent figure, whether or not he is threatened by his enemy.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Pyrotechnics}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The smoke effect of this spell blocks lines of sight and hinders missile fire just like magical mists or fogs.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Shocking Grasp}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par At the DM's option, creatures in metal armor may be considered AC 10 versus the wizard's touch. Only Dexterity and magical adjustments apply.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Slow}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Creatures affected by a }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 slow}{\fs24\cgrid0  spell automatically lose initiative and reduce their base initiative and weapon speeds by two phases. As noted in the spell description, their movement rates and attack rates are halved.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Priest Spells
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Few priest spells in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Handbook}{\fs24\cgrid0  that do no
t duplicate wizard's spells actually affect the battlefield to any significant degree. However, the Tome of Magic includes a number of priest spells in the sphere of war designed for the battlefield.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Air Walk}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Creatures affected by an }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 air walk}{\fs24\cgrid0  spell move a
nd fight as if they were on solid ground as opposed to engaging in true aerial combat. They threaten squares in front of them and can guard against the attacks of other flying creatures, gaining the advantages of that combat action.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Obscurement
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Like magical fog, obscurement blocks lines of sight through the affected area, screening creatures in or behind the mists from most missile attacks.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Magical Items}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par There are hundreds of magical items that can create various effects on the battlefield. The exact resu
lts of a magical item's use are left to the DM's discretion, but here are some general guidelines on some specific items. In cases where a magical item duplicates a spell effect, refer the spell sections.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Potion of Growth
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Characters may easily change size by imbibing a }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 potion of growth}{\fs24\cgrid0 ; they gain the advantages of extended reach and size bonuses in overbearing or overruns as normal for monsters of that size.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Potion of Invulnerability}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The imbiber can still be knocked down, grappled, or overborne by creatures he is otherwise immune to.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Apparatus of Kwalish}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The mechanical claw attacks of the apparatus act in the slow phase of initiative.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Boots of Speed}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The Armor Class bonus applies when the character chooses a half-move or full-move combat action. Going toe-to-toe in a melee doesn't allow the wearer of the boots to take advantage of their defensive properties, although he can always use the extra moveme
nt.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Boots of Striding and Springing}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par If the wearer strikes before his enemy because of a better action speed or because he won initiative, he may spring away as described in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 DMG}{\fs24\cgrid0  without provoking an attack of opportunity. The +1 AC bonus applies all the
 time, even when the user is mired in a stationary slugging match.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Crossbow of Accuracy}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Although the weapon treats all shots as short range, it still has medium and long ranges for purposes of armor penetration. See crossbows in Chapter Seven: Weapons and Armor.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Scimitar of Speed}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 scimitar of speed }{\fs24\cgrid0 is very fast, and its wielder always wins initiative if he declares a combat action that involves an attack with the weapon.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Short Sword of Quickness}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par See }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 scimitar of speed}{\fs24\cgrid0 , above. If two such weapons are involved in a single battle, their strikes are considered to be simultaneous.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Sword of Sharpness}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par The magical properties of }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 sharpness }{\fs24\cgrid0 override the normal critical hit procedure. It is still possible to achieve a normal critical hit by rolling an 18 or 19, even if it doesn't quite activate the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 sharpness 
}{\fs24\cgrid0 properties. If a limb is severed, consider the injury to be a critical one of the appropriate sort\emdash Chapter Six details the unpleasant effects of losing limbs.
\par 
\par }{\b\i\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Vorpal Sword}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par As with the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 sword of sharpness}{\fs24\cgrid0 , the properties of a }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 vorpal blade }{\fs24\cgrid0 
override the critical hit procedure. If the wielder rolls an 18 or higher that meets the criteria for a critical hit without invoking the vorpal power, a normal critical hit results.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6\cgrid0 Chapter Four:
\par Weapon Specialization & Mastery
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par In a desperate battle, the only thing that stands between a brave hero and a grisly end is his skill with blade or shield. Skill at personal combat is a valuable 
commodity in a fantasy setting; it's hard to imagine Conan meeting his better at swordplay, or the Gray Mouser encountering a foe swifter and more agile than himself.
\par \tab This chapter describes a revised weapon proficiency system that includes several grades 
of specialization as well as new uses for a weapon proficiency. This material is completely compatible with the character point proficiency system described in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Skills & Powers}{\fs24\cgrid0 
 book; the point system allows characters to purchase proficiency, expertise, specialization, or mastery in the same way that an AD&D character can spend proficiency slots to gain these benefits.
\par \tab A number of new uses for weapon proficiencies are also included in this chapter. Weapon groups, fighting styles, attack maneuvers, shield use, armor use, special talents, and dirty tricks can all be learned by spending weapon proficiency slots.

\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12\cgrid0 Weapon Proficiencies  }{\fs24\cgrid0 Anybody can pick up a sword and swing it in a menacing manner, but without proper training and technique it's impossible to 
use a weapon correctly. A character's weapon proficiencies represent weapons in which he has some degree of training. Usually, he can wield them without embarrassing himself.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Intelligence and Proficiencies}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Fighters, paladins, and rangers may apply their b
onus language slots for high Intelligence scores to any kind of weapon proficiency.  Characters of any other type can only use these extra proficiency slots to learn nonweapon proficiencies. This rewards fighters who chose brains over brawn by allowing th
em to compensate for shortcomings in physical attributes with the benefits of skill and training.
\par \tab Under this rule, the character's number of allowed languages simply represents the most languages she can ever learn. The character isn't assumed to begin pla
y knowing any extra languages; instead, she must use nonweapon proficiency slots to learn any extra languages she wants to speak. Characters can always speak their native tongue without spending any proficiency slots.
\par \tab Demihuman characters who are allowed 
to learn extra languages still have to spend slots learning these tongues. Their advantage lies in the fact that they can begin play with knowledge of these languages, because they had access to people who spoke them when they were growing up.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Proficiencies and the 
\par }{\i\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Skills & Powers }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Book
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 The }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option}{\fs24\cgrid0 
 character point system replaces the normal acquisition of proficiency slots. Characters may decide to learn a lot of skills by devoting their character points to a variety of proficiencies, or they may c
hoose to specialize in a few areas. Regardless of how they elect to spend their character points, the end results are the same: either they have a proficiency in something or they don't.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Weapon Groups}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Many weapons are very similar in construction and tech
niques of use; for example, using a bastard sword with one hand is not too much different from using a long sword. Both weapons are heavy, two-edged blades that rely on slashing or chopping strokes to cut through armor. All weapons are categorized in }{
\i\fs24\cgrid0 tight groups}{\fs24\cgrid0 , which are further organized into }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 broad groups}{\fs24\cgrid0 . The particular group a weapon belongs to is noted under the weapon characteristics in Chapter Seven.
\par \tab Tight groups serve two functions in game play. First of all, all weapons in a tight group are cons
idered to be related to each other. A character who is proficient in one weapon belonging to a tight group is automatically familiar with the other weapons of that tight group and has a reduced penalty for nonproficiency when using them.
\par \tab Second, characters can learn to use all weapons in a tight group with a }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 weapon group proficiency}{\fs24\cgrid0 
. Weapon group proficiencies cost two slots, but may include a number of weapons. For example, a character could use a weapon group proficiency to gain proficiency in crossbows;
 by spending two slots, he actually gains proficiency in six different weapons.
\par \tab Some tight weapon groups are further organized into broad groups. For example, the tight groups of axes, picks, hammers, and maces are all part of the hafted weapon broad grou
p. Proficiency in all of the weapons of a broad weapon group can be learned for three proficiency slots.
\par \tab A number of weapons are completely unrelated to anything else. For example, lassoes just don't work like any other type of weapon. The same applies for nets, whips, bolas, and mancatchers. These weapons must be learned one at a time.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Specialization and Weapon Groups}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Having a weapon group proficiency counts as the first slot of specialization for one weapon in that group. When the character chooses to s
pecialize, he need only spend one additional proficiency slot and select any weapon of that group as his specialty. For example, a character with the crossbow tight group proficiency could spend one additional slot to specialize in the medium crossbow. He
's spent a total of three slots but has proficiency with six types of crossbows and specialization with his preferred weapon, the medium crossbow.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Character Classes and 
\par Weapon Proficiencies
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Most character classes are limited in their selection of weapons. However, in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Skills & Powers }{\fs24\cgrid0 
book, a character may choose to be proficient in a weapon she normally would not be allowed to use. This is reflected by requiring the character to spend more character points than normal to become proficient.
\par \tab As an optional rule, characters may learn to use barred weapons by paying extra proficiency slots. A rogue or priest may learn a weapon normally reserved for warriors by suffering a one-slot penalty, and
 wizards may learn weapons normally reserved for priests or rogues with a one-slot penalty. If a wizard wishes to learn the use of a weapon normally reserved for warriors only, she must pay a two-slot penalty. For example, a wizard who wants to use a long
 sword could do so by paying two weapon proficiencies for it, since long swords are available for rogues\emdash but she would have to pay three slots to become proficienct in the two-handed sword.
\par \tab Note that the limited number of weapon proficiencies available for nonwarrior characters will tend to control character abuse of this rule. Priests may be }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 capable }{\fs24\cgrid0 
of learning any weapons under these rules, but priests who use barred weapons do so at the risk of angering their superiors or deity.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 Kits and Barred Weapons}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Some kits may allow characters to use barred weapons; for example, militant wizards may select a sword as one of their weapon proficiencies. Generally, the bonuses and perks of a kit are considered to negate any out-of-class penalties. However, priests ma
y still be limited by their priesthood weapon restrictions.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6\cgrid0 New Weapons}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par A number of new weapons are introduced in Chapter Seven: Weapons and Armor. Many of these are similar to weapons that were restricted in the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Handbook}{\fs24\cgrid0 . The Weapon Table in Chap
ter Seven lists any classes that cannot use a particular weapon under normal circumstances.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Shield Proficiency}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par By spending a weapon proficiency, characters can become more skilled in the use of their shield. Modern re-enactments of medieval tournaments ha
ve demonstrated that the shield is a very important part of a warrior's protection. The extra protection conferred by the shield varies by the exact type the character becomes proficient in:
\par 
