\fs24\cgrid0 A free unarmed punch or kick.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24\cgrid0 
\par \tab For example, specialization in fighting with matched sticks or nunchuks may confer the negation of the normal  \endash 2/\endash 4 attack penalty for fighting with two weapons. R
apier and main-gauche specialization may provide the character with an extra block or trap maneuver with his main-gauche. The DM is the final judge of what bonus a particular style provides and whether or not a character can specialize in it.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Special Talents}{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par Weapon proficiencies can also be used to acquire a variety of special perks, traits, and characteristics useful for a fighter. Some of these talents were originally presented as nonweapon proficiencies and are noted with an asterisk; they can be purcha
sed with either type of proficiency slot.
\par \tab As always, the DM is the final arbiter of whether or not a particular option or ability is permitted in his campaign. If he decides that an ability doesn't fit, he can require the players to choose another talent.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Alertness*}{\fs24\cgrid0  (1 slot/6 CP)\tab Wisdom/Intuition, +1
\par Groups: All
\par \tab Some characters are unnaturally alert and instinctively note signs of trouble that other characters may miss. A character with this proficiency reduces his chance of being surprised by 1 in 10 if
 he makes a successful proficiency check. In situations where surprise is automatic, the character may still attempt a proficiency check. If he passes, he is surprised at the normal chance instead of automatically.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Ambidexterity }{\fs24\cgrid0 (1 slot/4 CP)\tab Dexterity/Aim
\par Groups: Warrior, Rogue
\par \tab Ambidextrous characters are able to use either hand with equal coordination and skill. They are neither right-handed nor left-handed. When fighting in two-weapon style, an ambidextrous character has two "primary" hands, and suffers a \endash 
2 penalty to hit with either weapon. If the ambidextrous character spends a slot to specialize in two-weapon fighting style, he suffers no penalty to attacks with either weapon.
\par 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\b\fs24\cgrid0 Ambush }{\fs24\cgrid0 (1 slot/4 CP)\tab Intelligence/Reason
\par Groups: Warrior, Rogue\tab Initial rating: 5
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {\fs24\cgrid0 \tab 
A character with this proficiency is skilled at laying ambushes and setting up surprise attacks. Most characters can set up an adequate ambush when the terrain favors it and they know the enemy is coming, but a character who spends a slot on thi
s skill is able to create ambushes where ambushes wouldn't normally be possible.
\par \tab Ambushes are impossible if the attackers have already been spotted by the victims; there's no point in hiding then. If the ambushing party knows their quarry is coming to the
m, they can lay an ambush. If the attack is going to take place in difficult or unusual circumstances, a proficiency check may be called for; failure indicates that the victims have spotted the ambush before they walk into it. Otherwise, the ambush is gua
ranteed to achieve surprise.
\par 
