\par }{\fs36\cf6\cgrid0 Chapter Seven:
\par Weapons & Armor
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright {
Characters are defined not only by their abilities and alignment, but also by their possessions and equipment. With the proper preparation and gear, there's almost nothing a daring hero can't accomplish.
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx270\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\fs24\cgrid0 \tab This chapter presents a comprehensive listing of ju
st about any kind of weapon or armor a character would ever want to use. The list is divided into nine different semi-historical groupings to help preserve consistency and accuracy. For example, the most sophisticated armor available during Western Europe
's Dark Ages was chain mail; plate mail hadn't been invented yet. If a particular nation or culture in your campaign has a Dark Ages flavor to it, armor heavier than chain mail shouldn't be available there.
\par \tab The equipment categories are Stone Age, Bronze Age, Roman, Dark Ages, Crusades, Renaissance, English Civil War, Middle Eastern, and Oriental. 
\par \tab The second purpose of this chapter is to update weapon and armor statistics for the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option}{\fs24\cgrid0  combat system. In addition to the standard size, type, and da
mage listings, a new number has been introduced: the knockdown die. Speed factors are printed as both a number and a category, for use in a standard AD&D game or with the }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 Player's Option}{\fs24\cgrid0 
 initiative rules. Partial armor tables are included, so that players can re-create historical or fantastic styles such as back-and-breast plate with greaves, chainmail shirts, and so on.
\par \tab Last but not least, a complete explanation of special weapon properties and encumbrance is included at the end of this chapter.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12\cgrid0 Equipment Groups  }{\fs24\cgrid0 
As long as people have been making weapons, different cultures have produced different types of armaments. One wouldn't expect to find an English longbow in ancient Greece, or a samurai's katana in medieval Venice. Of course, many AD&D campaigns 
aren't very "realistic," but even in the most cosmopolitan settings there will be local standards of technology and preferred weapons and equipment.
\par \tab It's always possible for rare or unusual weapons to trickle into an area from outside. For example, if a t
ribe of jungle savages has been trading with a more advanced culture, it's quite likely that they may have learned metalworking or at least be armed with steel spearheads and arrowheads they traded for. Despite this, players should try to equip their char
a
cters in a reasonable and consistent fashion. A Viking-like barbarian from the far north has no business beginning a campaign equipped with a blowgun, katana, or scimitar. It's far more reasonable for the character to wear chain mail and carry a throwing 
axe and long sword. The DM is the final judge of what a character can reasonably expect to be able to find in any particular area.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Reading the Equipment Lists
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 The culture lists include the following information:
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Name: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Most weapons have been described by their common AD&D equivalent, so a }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 flamberge }{\fs24\cgrid0 or }{\i\fs24\cgrid0 zweihander }{\fs24\cgrid0 
is simply referred to as a two-handed sword. There are some exceptions\emdash for example, the gladius, which is just a short sword. The individual weapon descriptions that follow the tables make note of any additional names by which the weapon is known.

\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 \tab Cost: }{\fs24\cgrid0 Each weapon has a listed cost based on the standard AD&D monetary system. Many weapons have different costs on different tables; these are weapons that are rarer in the one setting than the other. If
 the weapon has a "\emdash " for cost, it is effectively free, since it can easily be made by any character.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6\cgrid0 Stone Age or Savage Cultures
\par }{\fs24\cgrid0 Stone Age cultures are rare in most AD&D campaigns. Only the most isolated areas can be considered to be in the Stone Age. In our own history, most Stone Age cultures disappeared thousands of years ago\emdash 
but those that did survive still live today, in the remote areas of Malaysia and South America.
\par \tab Just because a culture uses Stone Age technology doesn't mean that it is primitiv
e or barbaric. Native Americans enjoyed complex, advanced societies without metal tools or weapons. Therefore, this category can also be used to describe Aztec-like civilizations that exist as contemporaries of societies with more advanced technology.

\par \tab Sav
age settings are found in physically remote areas where primitive societies have gained access to metalworking technology. This is a good catch-all category for equipping barbaric jungle tribes, fierce nomads, or tribes of cannibals. Historically, many Af
rican, Asian, and Malaysian nations were considered "savages" by Western European explorers as late as the early part of this century. These unique cultures suffered terribly at the hands of their supposedly more-civilized visitors.
\par \tab Stone Age weapons inclu
de a variety of missiles developed from basic hunting tools. They tend to be inferior to even Bronze Age arms, which were designed as implements of war. Armor is light or nonexistent and consists of a hide-covered shield and leather, hide, or cord body ar
mor at best.
\par \tab Savage arms include improved metal-headed versions of many Stone Age weapons. Savage armor is also light, but includes copper or bronze reinforcement. In some cases, very sophisticated lamellar or scale armor of wood, bone, or leather may be a
vailable. Historically, chain mail appeared in the 18th and 19th century among Malaysian and Asian tribes, hundreds of years after Europeans abandoned its use.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cgrid0 Stone Age and Savage Settings
\par 
